package "Common Collector Hierarchy (every source category)" {
  abstract class SourceCollector {
    +collector_id : string
    +source_ref : string
    +collection_trigger : CollectionTrigger
    +collection_window : object
    +parameters : object
    +collect() : Result<vector<RawStateRecord>, CollectorError>
    +get_metadata() : collection_metadata
    +get_diagnostics() : diagnostics
    +health_probe() : health_status
  }

  abstract class SCoreCollector {
    +source_ref : string = "score.<module>"
    +collector_id : string = "<module>-collector"
    +transport : Transport = s-core-api
  }

  abstract class RuntimeCollector {
    +source_ref : string = "runtime.<metric>"
    +collector_id : string = "<metric>-collector"
  }

  abstract class HardwareCollector {
    +source_ref : string = "hardware.<device>"
    +collector_id : string = "<device>-collector"
  }

  note right of SourceCollector
    • Common VEL collector interface, identical for every
      source category (runtime, hardware, S-CORE module)
    • I/O API per source-collector-interface-design.md Sec 3
    • Lifecycle state machine per Sec 4.1
    • Error taxonomy per Sec 5.1
  end note

  note right of SCoreCollector
    • S-CORE-specific naming/transport conventions, layered
      on SourceCollector (see s-core-collector-common-design.md)
    • One subclass per S-CORE module; a module-specific
      concrete collector (e.g. LifecycleCollector) extends this
      further. See <module>-collector-design.md for each
      concrete collector.
  end note

  note right of RuntimeCollector
    • Runtime-metric-specific conventions (CPU, GPU, NPU
      utilization, e.g. vendor-a-npu), layered directly on
      SourceCollector -- not yet drafted
    • Sibling category to SCoreCollector and HardwareCollector,
      not a subclass of either
  end note

  note right of HardwareCollector
    • Hardware-data-specific conventions (sensor states,
      device status), layered directly on SourceCollector --
      not yet drafted
    • Sibling category to SCoreCollector and RuntimeCollector,
      not a subclass of either
  end note

  SourceCollector <|-- SCoreCollector : extends
  SourceCollector <|-- RuntimeCollector : extends
  SourceCollector <|-- HardwareCollector : extends
}

package "Shared Config-Driven State-Mapping Engine (reused by every collector)" {
  interface IStateFieldBindingProvider {
    +field_names() : list<string>
    +read_field(field_name : string) : any
    +subscribe(on_update : callback) : bool
  }

  class ConfigDrivenStateMapper {
    -schema_ref : RawEvidenceSchema
    +map_to_raw_record(bindings : IStateFieldBindingProvider) : object
    +validate_required_fields() : bool
  }

  class CollectorDiagnostics {
    +error_code : string
    +error_category : ErrorCategory
    +severity : Severity
    +message : string
    +retry_count : uint32
    +recovery_hint : string
    +emit() : AdapterDiagnostic
  }

  SourceCollector ..> IStateFieldBindingProvider : uses (via a module-specific adapter)
  SourceCollector ..> ConfigDrivenStateMapper : uses
  SourceCollector ..> CollectorDiagnostics : reports via
  ConfigDrivenStateMapper ..> IStateFieldBindingProvider : reads fields via

  note right of IStateFieldBindingProvider
    • ONE shared interface implemented by every module's thin
      field-binding adapter (e.g. LifecycleFieldBindings,
      PersistencyFieldBindings, a future NpuFieldBindings, ...)
    • Adding a new source requires implementing ONLY this
      interface + a new RawEvidenceSchema / EvidenceNormalizationRule
      config -- no new Mapper, Validator, or Normalizer class
    • read_field() covers synchronous/pull-based sources; subscribe()
      covers event-driven/push-based sources (e.g. Lifecycle's
      external monitor notification capability) -- the adapter
      registers via subscribe() and, on each event, atomically swaps
      in one immutable snapshot of the full event payload; read_field()
      calls within one collection cycle all resolve against that
      same snapshot (never torn across two event generations)
    • subscribe() returns bool (true = this adapter has an
      event-driven source and registered on_update; false = this
      adapter is pull-only and on_update will never be invoked).
      This lets the Runtime/Mapper call subscribe() uniformly on
      every adapter without assuming push support, instead of
      forcing pull-only adapters to implement a silent no-op.
    • any (read_field's return type) is implemented in C++ as
      std::any (or std::variant<T...> where the field-type set is
      closed); ConfigDrivenStateMapper knows each field's declared
      type from the RawEvidenceSchema and performs a checked
      std::any_cast<T>() (or std::get<T>()) against it, so type
      safety is recovered at the mapper boundary rather than in
      the interface signature itself.
  end note

  note right of ConfigDrivenStateMapper
    • Built ONCE, reused by every collector of every source category
    • Driven entirely by the source's RawEvidenceSchema config
      (field name / path / type / required)
    • Builds the schema-conformant raw_state_records generically by
      iterating schema fields and pulling values from the
      IStateFieldBindingProvider -- zero per-source mapper code
    • Pull vs push is NOT a concern of the mapper: read_field(name)
      may resolve against a synchronous API call made on the spot, or
      against a cached snapshot most recently delivered through
      subscribe(on_update) -- the adapter encapsulates which, and the
      mapper treats every field identically
  end note

  note right of CollectorDiagnostics
    • error_category: ErrorCategory enum (CONFIGURATION_ERROR ... INTERNAL_ERROR)
    • severity: Severity enum (INFO ... FATAL)
    • emit() produces AdapterDiagnostic
    • SHARED across all source categories
    • See source-collector-interface-design.md Sec 5
  end note
}

package "Enums" {
  enum CollectionTrigger {
    PERIODIC
    EVENT
    ON_DEMAND
  }

  enum Transport {
    S_CORE_API
    DDS
    FILE
    COMMAND
    OS_INTERFACE
  }

  enum ErrorCategory {
    CONFIGURATION_ERROR
    SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE
    AUTHENTICATION_ERROR
    TIMEOUT
    SCHEMA_VIOLATION
    NORMALIZATION_ERROR
    RESOURCE_EXHAUSTION
    INTERNAL_ERROR
  }

  enum Severity {
    INFO
    WARNING
    ERROR
    FATAL
  }
}

note bottom
  **Module-specific example (see Lifecycle_collector_class_diagram.puml):**
  LifecycleCollector extends SCoreCollector; LifecycleFieldBindings
  implements IStateFieldBindingProvider. These are the ONLY Lifecycle-specific
  classes -- ConfigDrivenStateMapper and CollectorDiagnostics above are reused
  unchanged.
end note

SourceCollector ..> CollectionTrigger : typed by
SCoreCollector ..> Transport : typed by
CollectorDiagnostics ..> ErrorCategory : typed by
CollectorDiagnostics ..> Severity : typed by

@enduml
